.TH "ELEMENTINTERNALS.LABELS" "3JS" "September 17, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
ElementInternals.labels \- ElementInternals: labels property
.SH SYNOPSIS
The \f[B]\f[CB]labels\f[B]\f[R] read\-only property of the
\f[CR]ElementInternals\f[R] interface returns the labels associated with
the element.
.SH VALUE
A \f[CR]NodeList\f[R] containing all of the label elements associated
with this element.
.SS Exceptions
.TP
\f[B]NotSupportedError\f[R] \f[B]DOMException\f[R]
Thrown if the element does not have its \f[CR]formAssociated\f[R]
property set to \f[CR]true\f[R].
.SH ACCESSIBILITY CONCERNS
A \f[CR]<label>\f[R] associated with a form\-associated custom element
is exposed to assistive technology in the same way as a label on a
built\-in form control.
In Chrome and Firefox it provides the accessible name for the element.
.PP
For a screen reader to reach that name, the element also has to be
focusable.
A custom element is not focusable by default.
It needs a \f[CR]tabindex\f[R] attribute, or a shadow root created with
\f[CR]delegatesFocus: true\f[R] and a focusable element inside it.
.PP
Safari does not expose the label this way.
VoiceOver does not read a \f[CR]<label>\f[R] linked to a
form\-associated custom element (\c
.UR https://webkit.org/b/259124
WebKit bug 259124
.UE \c
).
An element that relies on the label association alone therefore has no
accessible name in Safari.
.PP
To give the element an accessible name in every browser, set
\f[CR]ariaLabel\f[R] on the element\(cqs internals as well as
associating the label:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]class\f[R] CustomCheckbox \f[B]extends\f[R] HTMLElement {
  \f[B]static\f[R] formAssociated = \f[B]true\f[R];

  constructor() {
    \f[B]super\f[R]();
    \f[B]this\f[R].internals_ = \f[B]this\f[R].attachInternals();
    \f[B]this\f[R].internals_.role = \(dqcheckbox\(dq;
    \f[B]this\f[R].internals_.ariaLabel = \(dqJoin newsletter\(dq;
  }
}
.EE
.RS
.PP
\f[B]Note:\f[R] Setting \f[CR]ariaLabel\f[R] on the internals defines a
\f[I]default\f[R] semantic for the element.
An \f[CR]aria\-label\f[R] attribute set on the element itself takes
precedence over it, which lets a page author override the name without
the component losing its own fallback.
.RE
.SH EXAMPLES
The following example shows a custom checkbox component with a
\f[CR]<label>\f[R] element linked to it.
Printing the value of \f[CR]labels\f[R] to the console returns a
\f[CR]NodeList\f[R] with one entry, representing this label.
.IP
.EX
<\f[B]form\f[R] id=\(dqmyForm\(dq>
  <\f[B]custom\-checkbox\f[R] id=\(dqcustom\-checkbox\(dq tabindex=\(dq0\(dq></\f[B]custom\-checkbox\f[R]>
  <\f[B]label\f[R] for=\(dqcustom\-checkbox\(dq>Join newsletter</\f[B]label\f[R]>
</\f[B]form\f[R]>
.EE
.IP
.EX
\f[B]class\f[R] CustomCheckbox \f[B]extends\f[R] HTMLElement {
  \f[B]static\f[R] formAssociated = \f[B]true\f[R];

  constructor() {
    \f[B]super\f[R]();
    \f[B]this\f[R].internals_ = \f[B]this\f[R].attachInternals();
  }

  \f[I]// \&...\f[R]
}

window.customElements.define(\(dqcustom\-checkbox\(dq, CustomCheckbox);

\f[B]const\f[R] element = document.getElementById(\(dqcustom\-checkbox\(dq);
console.log(element.internals_.labels); \f[I]// NodeList [ label ]\f[R]
.EE
.RS
.PP
\f[B]Note:\f[R] A label is only included in \f[CR]labels\f[R] once it
has been parsed.
Reading \f[CR]labels\f[R] from \f[CR]connectedCallback()\f[R] returns an
empty \f[CR]NodeList\f[R] when the associated \f[CR]<label>\f[R] comes
after the element in the source, because the parser has not reached it
yet.
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]ElementInternals.ariaLabel\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]HTMLElement.attachInternals()\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
